We model the vibrating beam supported at two ends as a base excitation problem. Upon concentrating all of the mass of the beam at its center, the lagrangian of the system can be given as such:
\[
\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{2}m\dot{\bar x}^2-\frac{1}{2}k(\bar x-x_b)^2
\]
Likewise, the non-conservative dissipative function, which accounts for the damping of the mass $m$, can be given as such:
\[
D=-\frac{1}{2}R(\dot{\bar x}-\dot x_b)^2
\]
The EOM of the system is given as such (in accordance with Newton's 2nd Law):
\[
\frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\dot{\bar x}}-\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\bar x}=\frac{\partial D}{\partial\dot{\bar x}}\implies m\ddot{\bar x}+k(\bar x-x_b)=-R(\dot{\bar x}-\dot x_b)\implies\boxed{m\ddot{\bar x}+R\dot{\bar x}+k\bar x=R\dot x_b+kx_b\vphantom{\frac{1}{1}}}
\]
Upon defining $\omega_n=\sqrt{k/m}$ and $\zeta=R/2m\omega_n$, this can be equivalently formulated as follows:
\[
\boxed{\ddot{\bar x}+2\zeta\omega_n\dot{\bar x}+\omega_n^2\bar x=2\zeta\omega_n\dot x_b+\omega_n^2x_b\vphantom{\frac{1}{1}}}
\]
We can now identify the forcing function (per unit mass) of the system:
\[
f(t)=2\zeta\omega_n\dot x_b(t)+\omega_n^2x_b(t)
\]
With the understanding that the base displacement function can be expressed as a sinusoid that \textit{turns on} at $t=0$, we may say for some $A$ and $\omega_f$ (with $u(t)$ being the unit step function):
\[
x_b(t)=A\sin(\omega_ft)\,u(t)\implies f(t)=A(2\zeta\omega_n\omega_f\cos(\omega_f t)+\omega_n^2\sin(\omega_f t))\,u(t)
%A\omega_n\sqrt{(2\zeta\omega_f)^2+\omega_n^2}\sin\left(\omega_ft+\tan^{-1}\left(2\zeta\frac{\omega_f}{\omega_n}\right)\right)
\]
Now let $\beta=\zeta\omega_n$ and $\omega_d=\omega_n\sqrt{1-\zeta^2}$. It thus follows that the EOM and the forcing function (per unit mass) can be expressed as follows:
\[
\boxed{\ddot{\bar x}+2\beta\dot{\bar x}+\omega_n^2\bar x=f(t)\quad\text{with}\quad f(t)=A(2\beta\omega_f\cos(\omega_ft)+\omega_n^2\sin(\omega_ft))\,u(t)\vphantom{\frac{1}{1}}}
\]
The Green's Function for this system has the following form:
\[
G(t)=\frac{1}{\omega_d}e^{-\beta t}\sin(\omega_dt)u(t)
\]
Upon combining the general homogeneous solution to the system $x_h$ (i.e. $A=0$) to the particular solution generated by the Green's Function, the general solution to the forced system can be obtained as follows:
\begin{align*}
\bar x(t)&=\bar x_h(t)+\int_{-\infty}^\infty f(\tau)\,G(t-\tau)\,d\tau
\\[5 mm]
&=\bar x_h(t)+\int_{-\infty}^\infty A(2\beta\omega_f\cos(\omega_f\tau)+\omega_n^2\sin(\omega_f\tau))\,u(\tau)\,\frac{1}{\omega_d}e^{-\beta (t-\tau)}\sin(\omega_d(t-\tau))u(t-\tau)\,d\tau
\\[5 mm]
&=\bar x_h(t)+\frac{A}{\omega_d}\int_0^t (2\beta\omega_f\cos(\omega_f\tau)+\omega_n^2\sin(\omega_f\tau))\,e^{-\beta (t-\tau)}\sin(\omega_d(t-\tau))\,d\tau
\\[5 mm]
&=e^{-\beta t}\left(\bar x_0\cos(\omega_dt)+\frac{\dot{\bar x}_0+\beta\bar x_0}{\omega_d}\sin(\omega_dt)\right)
\\[3 mm]
&\qquad+\frac{A}{\omega_d}\int_0^t (2\beta\omega_f\cos(\omega_f\tau)+\omega_n^2\sin(\omega_f\tau))\,e^{-\beta (t-\tau)}\sin(\omega_d(t-\tau))\,d\tau
\end{align*}
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Therefore, the general solution to the EOM follows as such:
\vspace{3 mm}
\begin{align*}
\bar x(t)&=e^{-\beta t}\left(\bar x_0\cos(\omega_dt)+\frac{\dot{\bar x}_0+\beta\bar x_0}{\omega_d}\sin(\omega_dt)\right)
\\[3 mm]
&\qquad+\frac{A}{\omega_d}\int_0^t e^{-\beta (t-\tau)}(2\beta\omega_f\cos(\omega_f\tau)+\omega_n^2\sin(\omega_f\tau))\sin(\omega_d(t-\tau))\,d\tau
\end{align*}
\vspace{3 mm}\\
Now, consider the EOM in the frequency domain with $\hat{\bar x}=\mathscr{F}\{\bar x\}$ and $\hat x_b=\mathscr{F}\{x_b\}$:
\[
(-\omega^2+2\zeta\omega_n\omega i+\omega_n^2)\,\hat{\bar x}(\omega)=(2\zeta\omega_n\omega i+\omega_n^2)\,\hat x_b(\omega)
\]
The transfer function pertaining to this system thus has the following form:
\[
T(\omega)=\frac{\hat{\bar x}(\omega)}{\hat x_b(\omega)}=\frac{1+2\zeta\left(\frac{\omega}{\omega_n}\right)i}{1-\left(\frac{\omega}{\omega_n}\right)^2+2\zeta\left(\frac{\omega}{\omega_n}\right)i}\implies\left|T(\omega)\right|
=
\sqrt{\frac{1+4\zeta^2\left(\frac{\omega}{\omega_n}\right)^2}{\left(1-\left(\frac{\omega}{\omega_n}\right)^2\right)^2+4\zeta^2\left(\frac{\omega}{\omega_n}\right)^2}}
\]
Define the auxiliary function:
\[
y(x)\coloneq\left|T\left(\omega_n\sqrt{x}\right)\right|^2=\frac{1+ax}{(1-x)^2+ax}\quad\text{with}\quad a=4\zeta^2
\]
It follows that:
\[
y'(x)=\frac{-ax^2-2x+2}{((1-x)^2+ax)^2}\implies\left(y'(x_{\text{max}})=0\implies -ax_{\text{max}}^2-2x_{\text{max}}+2=0\right)
\]
Furthermore, the (local) maximum value of $y$ occurs when $y'=0$ and $y''<0$. This occurs specifically when:
\[
x_{\text{max}}=\frac{\sqrt{1+2a}-1}{a}\implies y_{\text{max}}=y(x_{\text{max}})=\frac{(\sqrt{1+2a}+1)^2}{(\sqrt{1+2a}-1)(\sqrt{1+2a}+3)}
\]
It holds that $y_{\text{max}}=|T|^2_{\text{max}}$. If we define $M\coloneq\sqrt{y_{\text{max}}}$, which corresponds to the* (maximum) peak of the modulus $|T(\omega)|$, then it follows that:
\[
M^2=\frac{(\sqrt{1+2a}+1)^2}{(\sqrt{1+2a}-1)(\sqrt{1+2a}+3)}\implies a=\frac{2M}{\sqrt{M^2-1}}\left(\frac{M}{\sqrt{M^2-1}}-1\right)
\]
Upon recalling that $a=4\zeta^2$, we can finally obtain:
\[
\boxed{\;\zeta=\sqrt{\frac{M}{2\sqrt{M^2-1}}\left(\frac{M}{\sqrt{M^2-1}}-1\right)}\;}
\]
\begin{center}
    The Damping Ratio
\end{center}
